From b600eab38867d2eacfb8a9a1a4efa51b93468aab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 16:56:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(areas): computed local depth on the 32 city hubs, and
 two Stage 6 fixes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Stage 6 left two defects that only showed up in the rendered output.

1. Thirty-two city pages drew worked-example rings that nothing on the page
   named. /areas/barrie rendered nine pulsing rings, none of them in Barrie,
   with neither the names nor SHOWCASE_DISCLOSURE anywhere in the HTML. A ring
   reads as a claim; an unexplained ring reads as a claim about the town you
   are looking at.

   Fixed at the root: shownShowcase() is now one rule used by both the map and
   the crawlable strip, and `focus` narrows it to that municipality. Barrie
   draws nothing and says nothing. Toronto draws one grouped marker badged 4.

   tests/showcase.test.ts passed the whole time because it asserted the
   disclosure existed in a FILE. The new assertion is about every page that
   draws a pin, which is what the rule always meant.

2. The legend repeated the strip's heading verbatim, and the legend is
   server-rendered whenever it starts open — so /areas, /stairs and the eight
   service pages each shipped the same names twice and a screen reader read
   the list through twice. The legend list is a control; it now reads "Jump
   the map to" and carries its own aria-labelledby.

Then the substantive work. The 32 hub pages rank for "hardwood flooring in
{city}", the highest commercial-intent query this shop has, and were
rendering 308-420 words of main content.

The standard fix is four paragraphs of local colour per town. That is
inventing knowledge about places Franco knows and I do not, and 32
paraphrases of the same paragraphs is the definition of a doorway page. So
the depth is derived instead:

  LocalPriceTable       all six priceable services, banded by that city's
                        own multiplier through calculateEstimate
  JobTypesHere          the catalogue archetypes actually offered there,
                        read off the coverage tier (12 core, 7 extended)
  NearbyMunicipalities  the six nearest served towns, haversine

981-1,217 words, median 1,030. Every figure is checkable against /estimate,
and tests/city-depth.test.ts asserts it against the estimator rather than a
snapshot. No new URLs — the matrix stays frozen.

The neighbour block is the first sideways link between city hubs; all 32
previously linked upward to /areas and nowhere else. 192 proximity-ordered
body links, reciprocal enough to be a graph rather than a star.

Rendering bands per square foot next to the published ranges exposed a
disagreement that has been in the estimator since it was written and was
never shown in comparable units: installation prices above its published
$11-$22/sq ft ceiling in all 32 municipalities. Both numbers are honest and
measure different things — the published range spans every specification,
the table prices one, and not the cheap one — and the page now says so and
names the cheap end. Closing the gap means either raising the ceiling or
making the estimator's default less rich, and both are the shop setting its
own prices, so it is written up as blocker #11 with the one-line change for
each. The drift is bounded meanwhile: nothing may exceed a published ceiling
by more than 15%, nothing may fall below a floor, and only install and deck
may sit outside at all.

Playwright caught one layout bug: the price table propagated its width up
through a grid item whose default min-width:auto stopped the scroll container
from scrolling, giving 242-312px of horizontal page overflow on a phone.
min-w-0 on the column, and the specification moved under the service name so
the timeline stops clipping.

308 tests pass (290 before). 393 prerendered pages, unchanged. Audit clean.
13 routes x 6 viewports, no horizontal overflow.

